ation master so the factory key 0 can never reopen a shipped sticker. Scheme id an10922-aes128/meta:batch+file:uid/v1 travels in every receipt. tap.ts now identifies the tag first (plain: UID in the URL; encrypted: try each batch's meta key until one decrypts to a registered UID) and only then verifies with that tag's own keys. sdmurl.ts builds the NDEF URL template and derives every mirror offset from the placeholder positions; sunsim.ts fills a template the way a chip does and pushes it through the real verifier — a one-byte slip in either offset fails the test (13 tests). ev2.mjs (session keys, command/response MACs, ChangeKey cryptogram with JAM CRC, ChangeFileSettings SDM payload, NDEF framing, APDUs) is pure and self-tested with 23 checks against RFC 4493 CMAC vectors etc.; reader.mjs drives an ACR122U through nfc-pcsc (installed beside the lane, lazily imported). Per tag: UID + GetVersion (part check), derive, authenticate with factory key 0, write NDEF, enable SDM, change keys 1, 2, then 0 last, READ BACK and verify through verifyTap, seal tag.program (uid, batch, role, part, bench, key_derivation, key fingerprints not keys, url_template_sha256, offsets, readback counter+loop, serial), append lanes/nfc/batches/<batch>.jsonl. Refuses a production batch with missing or all-zero masters before opening the reader; --bench is the only way past and is recorded. HONEST LIMIT: no reader here and NXP publishes no AuthenticateEV2First vector, so ev2.mjs has never met a chip; the read-back gate is what stands in for that, and the README says program one before ten. Template for v.vlt.to: url offsets picc 21 mac 56 tt 76, file offsets 20/55/75, 77-byte NDEF, template sha aed10a4c…. (3) RUNBOOK docs/runbooks/first-seal.md for hands: what to buy (TagTamper stock, ACR122U, phones), program stickers, commit the box WITH THE LID OPEN (`timmy custody commit --serial --contents`, new lanes/custody/commit.mjs seals custody.commit with the contents hash and keeps the manifest be